To compute the second‑year depreciation we follow the policy step by step.

**1. First‑year (prorated) depreciation**

Purchase price \(P = \$80{,}000\)  
Salvage value \(S = \$9{,}000\)  
Depreciation rate \(= \frac{2}{5}=0.40\)

The asset is in service for 7 months of the fiscal year, so the first‑year expense is

\[
E_1 = 0.40 \times P \times \frac{7}{12}
     = 0.40 \times 80{,}000 \times \frac{7}{12}
     = 32{,}000 \times \frac{7}{12}
     = \frac{224{,}000}{12}
     = \$18{,}666.\overline{6}
\]

**2. Beginning book value for year 2**

\[
BV_{\text{start,\,yr2}} = P - E_1
                        = 80{,}000 - 18{,}666.\overline{6}
                        = \$61{,}333.\overline{3}
\]

**3. Second‑year depreciation**

The same rate of 40 % applies to the beginning book value:

\[
E_2 = 0.40 \times BV_{\text{start,\,yr2}}
    = 0.40 \times 61{,}333.\overline{3}
    = 24{,}533.\overline{3}
\]

The book value after year 2 would still be well above the salvage value, so no adjustment is needed.

**4. Round to nearest dollar**

\(E_2 \approx \$24{,}533\).

---

