The first‑year depreciation is prorated because the equipment was in service only 10 months:

\[
\text{First‑year depreciation} = \frac{2}{5}\times \$53,000\times\frac{10}{12}
= 0.4 \times 53{,}000 \times \tfrac{5}{6}
= 21{,}200 \times \tfrac{5}{6}
= \$17,666.\overline{66}
\]

Book value at the end of year 1:

\[
BV_{Y1}=53{,}000-17{,}666.\overline{66}=35{,}333.\overline{33}
\]

For year 2, apply the 40 % declining‑balance rate to the beginning book value.  
The depreciation amount would be:

\[
\text{Second‑year depreciation}=0.4 \times 35{,}333.\overline{33}
= \$14{,}133.\overline{33}
\]

Since this does not reduce the book value below the salvage value of \$6,500, no adjustment is needed.

